<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel López de Legazpi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1502–<a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel López de Legazpi was a Spanish conquistador who led the expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> that began the Spanish colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1565. He founded the first Spanish settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>, moved his base to Panay in 1569 and in 1571 took Maynila, founding Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> as the capital. He was the first governor-general of the Philippines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi.jpg" alt="Miguel López de Legazpi" width="320" height="525" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Celestí Sadurní i Deop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miguel_L%C3%B3pez_de_Legazpi,_en_La_Hormiga_de_Oro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miguel López de Legazpi was born in Zumarraga in the Basque province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gipuzkoa">Guipúzcoa</a>. His family was wealthy and held military and municipal offices, and his father, Juan de Legazpi, was a soldier who had served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. His education is unknown, but his later posts suggest legal training. After his father's death in 1527 his older brother inherited the family wealth.</p><p>Around 1528 he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. He served as clerk of the mint, secretary of the municipal council and, in 1559, alcalde mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and he took part in several inquisitorial processes between 1536 and 1543. In 1532 he married Isabel Garcés, with whom he had nine children. Two grandsons, Felipe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salcedo">Juan de Salcedo</a>, later sailed with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1564 the viceroy Luís de Velasco commissioned him, on the order of King Philip II, to lead an expedition across the Pacific. Five ships and about 500 soldiers sailed from Barra de Navidad in November 1564, with the friar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-urdaneta">Andrés de Urdaneta</a> as navigator and spiritual adviser. After 93 days they reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a>, where they fought the Chamorro. In the Philippines Legazpi made blood compacts with local chiefs, including Datu Sikatuna on 16 March 1565, and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> on 27 April 1565, where he overcame Rajah Tupas and founded a settlement.</p><p>A Portuguese blockade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> in 1568 failed. In 1569 food shortages led him to move to Panay. In 1570 he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-goiti">Martín de Goiti</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, and Goiti's force defeated a fleet led by Bambalito at the Bangkusay Channel. Legazpi followed, made a pact with the local rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a> and Lakan Dula, and established a settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> on 24 June 1571. He ordered the walled city of Intramuros built and proclaimed Manila the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Legazpi governed the islands for about a year. He died of a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> on 20 August 1572, bankrupt after spending most of his fortune on the conquest, and was buried in San Agustin Church in Intramuros. His letters to Philip II on the expedition are kept in the General Archive of the Indies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><p>His expedition with Urdaneta effectively created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a> trade, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> was exchanged for Chinese silk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and other goods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> remained a Spanish possession until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 10 December 1898, apart from the British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and Cavite in 1762 to 1764. The capital of Albay province bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel López de Legazpi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brunei's power in the Philippines was challenged by Spain, whose forces under <b>Miguel López de Legazpi</b> took Manila from its Muslim rulers, relatives and allies of Brunei, in 1570–1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</b> arrived from New Spain in 1565, founded a settlement at Cebu and in 1571 took the Kingdom of Maynila, making Manila the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</b> moved his base to Panay in 1569 and Spanish rule followed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedatuan-of-madja-as">Kedatuan of Madja-as</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1571 <b>Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</b> occupied the site and founded Spanish Manila.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maynila">Kingdom of Maynila</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</b> took Maynila in 1571, Lakandula accepted Spanish rule and was baptised.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tondo">Kingdom of Tondo</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</b> arrived in 1565 and Humabon's successor Rajah Tupas submitted to Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/rajahnate-of-cebu">Rajahnate of Cebu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zumarraga, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,-2.32&amp;year=1547" aria-label="Zumarraga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Miguel López de Legazpi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4855 3889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4855 3889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,-2.32&amp;year=1547">What was here in 1547? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
